  <context>
    <background>🔴 THE OWNER QUESTION IS ANSWERED. It was open when this file was a stub; decision D-2 (2026-09-18, confirmed 2026-09-19 "follow recommendation") settled all three parts:

- **Part 1 = YES.** The ADDITIVE path is bound on `sprk_enddate`: a date-ended membership stops conferring, and access must be re-granted explicitly. The owner's words: "if an external user is removed from an organization then that external user must be reassigned access."
- **Part 2 = NO.** The deny-veto subject stays on `statecode` ALONE and deliberately OVER-matches — an org-keyed ethical wall KEEPS binding a former member. Support: FR-23 (`spec.md:86`), `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:550-554` "Denial deliberately OVER-matches."
- **Part 3.** Count and list date-ended-but-active rows BEFORE deploy; shipping part 1 removes live access.
- **Part 4.** The deactivation WRITER is task 117 (D-1 option B), designed once and shared.

WHY THIS FILE DOES NOT IMPLEMENT ANYTHING. The dilemma recorded on this task — "one query serves an additive term and a veto subject, so one filter shape cannot be right for both" — DISSOLVED: it was an artifact of projecting bare ids. It is one READ, not one FILTER. ISS-019's suggested fix was a SECOND query (`QueryActiveOrgIdsOutcomeAsync`) for the veto path, which would restore the two-snapshot hazard. So 109 and 110 are ONE change, and it is task 109 — which also carries ISS-019 and ISS-026's read guard.

⚠️ The stale premises this stub used to assert have been removed: the `&lt;owner-question status="UNDECIDED"&gt;` block (now answered), and the claim that "exposure is nil because zero organisations hold a standing grant." That claim was FALSE — only the org-EXPANSION term is standing-gated (`ExternalParticipationService.cs:1209-1221`); the older org-GRANT term (`QueryOrganizationGrantRowsAsync`, `:1011-1051`) has no standing check at all, and live org grants existed (`notes/task-020-org-grant-spe-cleanup.md:18`). Real exposure was bounded only by dev being a test environment.</background>
